## 1. TL;DR — what Trellis is

**Trellis is a generative-UI agentic coding planner.** You describe code work at any
granularity ("tighten this validator", "add OAuth login", "extract billing into a service",
"migrate REST → gRPC across the monorepo"); Trellis produces an **interactive dependency
graph** of the changes, where each node carries **grounded engineering analysis** (assumptions,
risks, benefits, notable symbols) cited to real symbols in your repo. You can then:

- **Inspect** each node with a change-type-specific generated widget (schema diff, API
  contract, component preview, call-graph impact…).
- **Iterate** by adding context — the plan re-flows in real time as a new revision.
- **Operate** — dispatch independent branches **in parallel** to a coding agent (Claude Code,
  headless), each on its own isolated git worktree; diffs stream back live; an **integration
  node** reconverges branches behind a test gate.
- **Delegate** — prune a subtree, export it as a portable spec, and hand it to another user or
  agent with a role (viewer / runner / editor).

The product's one-line thesis:

> **The agent's output is software, not an answer.** Every coding assistant returns a chat
> reply or a diff. Trellis returns a third thing: **a plan you can operate.**

It is positioned as **"GitHub for plans"**: decompose → delegate → reconverge.

