## 18. Execution, worktrees, drift & integration

### Isolation model

- **One git worktree per node** (`createWorktree()` off `base_commit`, branch `trellis/<id>`).
  Parallel builds physically cannot collide — different directories. Orchestration owns the
  merge.
- **`ensureRepo()`** clones the repo (`--depth 50`, GitHub token injected for https) or, on any
  failure (no URL, clone error), generates a tiny committed **sample repo** so the pipeline
  always runs (degrade-never-crash).
- Worktrees are **GC'd** after each node run (`git worktree remove --force` + `rm -rf`).

### The Claude Code runner

Spawns the `claude` binary headless in the worktree cwd:

```
claude -p "<prompt>" --output-format stream-json --verbose \
  --permission-mode <acceptEdits> --max-turns <N> --model <claude-sonnet-4-6>
```

It injects a **`CLAUDE.md`** into the worktree with: the goal, the **touch-set** (allowed
files/symbols/signatures — *edit ONLY these*), assumptions, risks, and acceptance commands.
The stream-json is parsed into `RunnerEvent`s (`text`, `tool_call`, `file_edit`, `token_usage`,
`status`) pushed to the run's Redis stream. A wall-clock guard (default 900s) SIGKILLs.
**If the binary is missing or spawn fails → falls back to the `NativeRunner` stub.**

### Drift detection

After a run, orchestration (not the runner) diffs the worktree and computes **drift = files
touched outside `allowed_files`** (excluding `CLAUDE.md`). Drift records a `node.drift` event
and a `status:drift` stream event. *(Design intent: drift re-derives affected edges/branches and
demotes a falsely-parallel branch to sequential before any corrupt merge — the safety story in
motion. The detection + signalling is built; automated re-derivation is the planned next step.)*

### Integration & merge

Branches reconverge at an **integration node**: sequential `git merge --no-ff`, then the full
test gate. **Nothing auto-merges on red** — a textual conflict or a red test writes a
`conflict_report` (kind `textual`/`semantic`) and leaves the plan `partially_merged`.

### Locks (Redis)

`keys.lockPlan/lockBranch/lockNode/lockFile`. The integration worker uses `lock:plan:{id}`. The
design also specifies `lock:file:{project}:{path}` as a parallel-safety backstop (serialize on
contention with a visible reason) for the native runner.

