## 20. The generative-UI system (two axes)

This is the assessed centerpiece (P4). "Generative" here is **structured & validated**, not
free-form HTML.

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  subgraph AxisA["Axis A — granularity → canvas layout"]
    A1[G1 → checklist]
    A2[G2 → compact_dag]
    A3[G3 → swimlane_dag]
    A4[G4 → hierarchical_map]
  end
  subgraph AxisB["Axis B — change_type → node-body widget"]
    B1[migration → schema_diff]
    B2[api_contract → api_contract]
    B3[ui_component → component_preview]
    B4[logic/refactor → call_graph_impact]
    B5[config → key_diff]
  end
  Planner -->|emits LayoutSpec| AxisA
  Analysis -->|emits WidgetSpec per node| AxisB
  AxisA --> Render["Trusted client registry<br/>(zod-validated, size-capped,<br/>no eval / no raw HTML)"]
  AxisB --> Render
```

- **Axis A** — the **planner** emits a `LayoutSpec` (tier, canvas, direction, grouping,
  parallelism_ui, delegation_ui, semantic_zoom, default_inspector_tab); the canvas renders the
  matching layout strategy. Layout = `f(granularity × change_type × context)`.
- **Axis B** — the **analysis agent** emits `WidgetSpec[]` per node; the client registry
  validates and renders the trusted component for the node's change type.

Trust & safety of generation: every spec is validated against zod schemas at the boundary;
props are byte-capped per widget; any failure degrades to a `FallbackWidget` with sanitized
plain text. Grounding is structurally guaranteed (every widget frame renders a `GroundingChip`),
and confidence < 0.5 surfaces a low-confidence banner.

