## 28. End-to-end request lifecycles (flows A–D)

**Flow A — Create a plan.** `POST /v1/plans` → insert `plans` (status `planning`) → enqueue
`plan-build` → worker: clone/index repo, run planner (Opus), resolve touch-sets, derive
edges/branches, persist nodes/edges/branches (status `ready`), enqueue one `analysis` job per
node → analysis worker upserts `node_annotations`. The canvas (already open via Realtime) fills
in live.

**Flow B — Run nodes/branches.** `POST /v1/plans/:id/run` (or `/branches/:id/run`) → insert a
`runs` row per node + enqueue `node-run` → worker creates a worktree, builds a `WorkOrder`, runs
Claude Code, streams events to `stream:run:{id}`, audits drift, sets node `built|blocked|failed`.
The browser tails the SSE stream into the run overlay. When branches are `built`, an
`integration` job merges them behind the test gate.

**Flow C — Re-plan (iterate).** `POST /v1/plans/:id/replan {context}` → compute next revision →
enqueue `replan`. *(Design: a replan agent writes a new `plan_revisions` row and re-derives only
the changed parts in < 8s. The queue + API + UI exist; the consuming worker is the pending
piece.)*

**Flow D — Delegate a subtree.** `POST /v1/plans/:id/delegate` → DFS the subtree → insert
`delegations` → upload the portable spec to Storage `specs` → update `spec_path` → insert a
paired `shares` grant. The recipient opens it as a runnable mini-plan; results merge back via the
integration node.

