## 30. Appendix — principles, competitive landscape, open questions

### Key design principles / ADRs

- **ADR — LLMs propose, deterministic services decide.** The model never has final say on a
  dependency (the safety core).
- **ADR-2 — durable vs ephemeral split.** Supabase = truth; Redis = control plane.
- **ADR-4 — grounding before generation.** Claims are derived against the real symbol graph
  before any generative UI is layered on top.
- **ADR-5 — no raw model HTML.** All generative UI renders from validated specs against a closed
  component registry.
- **Asymmetric caution.** A false dependency only costs parallelism; a false independence costs a
  corrupted merge — so when uncertain, assert a dependency.
- **Honesty constraints.** Independence is never presented as a guarantee; every analysis claim
  is grounded or labeled low-confidence; granularity range is bounded by value.

### Competitive positioning

Trellis's differentiator is the **triad running end-to-end**: a *ratifiable* parallel dependency
graph (not a linear plan) + per-context generative UI + subtree delegation. Linear agents
(Copilot Workspace, Cursor agent, Devin, Claude Code) produce a plan then build; Trellis produces
an **operable, parallelizable, delegable** plan. The economic claim is real wall-clock
parallelism (≥ 2.5× on G3) plus delegation of the most expensive labor an org has.

### Open questions (selected)

- **OQ — the exact FIR target** is the lone remaining P0 design question (the metric the product
  lives or dies on).
- CRDT/Yjs co-editing vs realtime + optimistic UI (deferred to post-v1).
- Whether agent-as-recipient delegation ships in MVP or Phase B.

